firing over the heads of the enemy is not useless.
you're telling your allies where to aim.

ideally, with tracer bullets, so they can see. Those work best at night.
aim at the point where the trails disappear, and/or where the smoke appears.

aim to the side, where your opponent isn't but where the flankers are.
use more ammunition than you think you need to. If there isn't always more, then you're losing.

Be brave. Advance. Fire where you know they should be, even if they're under cover.
Fire keeps them there. Long enough to let your allies get a different angle.
But, don't keep shooting if you aren't sure they're there. It's okay to fire in a direction to let
your allies know which direction is safe and which are dangerous. Bullets are bullets, they hurt all
those that they hit. [and those they are near]. Less "spray and pray", and more "put 3 bullets in
and around each window, that one seems to have been missed so I'll hit it just to be sure."

If there are civilians about, then you are either on the wrong side, or your foes are cruel bastards
who use people as shields. Sometimes, they have no choice. In that case, siege them out. Help the
people to escape. Treat them well, and fairly. Do not treat an unarmed soldier any differently than
a civilian.

Always be aware of what directions are safe, and which are hot. Use drones to direct fire using
lasers painted on the ground, and on surfaces that are upright that you can see. Works best in urban
environments, which are some of the hardest. A simple red/green line that says "safe to stand here"
and "always be firing when standing here" is a good line to have drawn in the sand. Use your guys
back home to fly the drones and paint those lines. Listen to comms, investigate, use the tech for
intelligence, not firepower.
